1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,680
So there's a group of economists-

3
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,040
Yeah, I said economists.

4
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:17,060
You know, the guys study the economy, tell us things about it.

5
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:19,540
Everybody knows an economist- right?

6
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,360
Well, they're climbing in the Alps and they get lost

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,260
And they're hopelessly lost.

8
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:29,480
They can't figure out where they are.

9
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,980
So they get out the map and they start studying it.

10
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:34,620
And they've got compasses and GPS units

11
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,920
And you know, they're taking bearings and looking at the sun and studying the sky and the landscape.

12
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,560
And finally one says: I think I know where we are,

13
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,580
I think I've got it.

14
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:47,019
And the others look at him and say okay,

15
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:50,240
And so he says: you see that mountain over there?

16
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:51,640
Yeah,

17
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:53,600
That mountain over there in the distance.

18
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:54,240
Yeah, that one.

19
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,780
Well, according to the map, we're standing on top of it.

20
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:04,940
All right,

21
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,120
This is podcast 45.

22
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:06,360
Hey,

23
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,160
Hey,

24
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:11,300
Hey,

25
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:38,179
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

26
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:39,460
This is Clark Green.

27
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:45,479
In our last podcast we talked about the concept of fun and that fun is challenge,

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,059
And I got this comment from Alan who said this

29
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,419
Regarding fun. I don't think we adults are always a good gauge of this.

30
00:01:52,420 --> 00:02:00,760
For instance, during our camp out this past weekend, I got the Scouts together for a night hike up a nearby short peak.

31
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,220
The other adults thought it would be a disaster in the dark, so they didn't go.

32
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,320
The full moon was shedding a surreal light over everything and the warm air was pleasant,

33
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:12,680
And the boys had to make their way through some thickets, over a ridge and then up to the summit.

34
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:17,620
I heard some discouraging words along the way and thought to myself: was this a mistake?

35
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,380
But once we reached the top, it really paid off.

36
00:02:21,519 --> 00:02:27,620
We looked out over beautiful natural vista and I could see that the boys were having fun,

37
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:30,480
And that's fun.

38
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:31,520
It wasn't easy

39
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,040
And the adults thought it was going to be a disaster,

40
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:41,820
But I think that's a great illustration, Alan.

41
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:48,240
I think that's a good illustration, Alan, of exactly what we were talking about,

42
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:58,300
And we need to continually challenge ourselves as adults to try and put ourselves in the mindset of the Scouts that we're working with.

43
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:03,280
We also talked about Roland Amundson's quote last week.

44
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:12,040
Roland Amundson, the polar explorer, who said that adventure is just a bit of bad planning.

45
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:19,680
A new Scoutmaster said this to me. I never knew I was in such great company.

46
00:03:20,739 --> 00:03:25,519
My personal life slogan is: it's not an adventure until something goes wrong.

47
00:03:28,019 --> 00:03:30,760
Well, in this podcast we're going to be talking about boards of review,

48
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:39,940
And I want to talk a little bit about how boards of review are conducted and give you some resources for training people who are going to sit on a board of review.

49
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:43,940
Before we move on, though, I want to talk about blogging and podcasting,

50
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:50,060
And I want to talk about that specifically in the context of Scout blogs and podcasts.

51
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:52,260
There's a handful of us out there,

52
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:56,240
And it takes a lot of time to put these things together,

53
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:59,660
And this is a little self-serving what I'm saying,

54
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:02,060
And I don't like to pay a whole lot of attention to it,

55
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,820
So you'll just forgive me for a moment,

56
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:09,840
But I thought it would be a good thing to explain exactly what this takes and how you can support it.

57
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:15,840
Besides myself, I'll give you, for instance, my buddy, Jerry, with the Scoutmaster Minute podcast.

58
00:04:16,399 --> 00:04:18,500
He's got a great podcast, great blog.

59
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,860
He's out there doing good work and certainly somebody who you should be listening to.

60
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:27,220
He is on something called PTC Media,

61
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:30,120
P-T-C Media.

62
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,920
Definitely worth going to the PTC Media website, listening to Jerry and the other podcasters there,

63
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:40,920
This blog and podcast. well, the blog started five years ago this month,

64
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:49,080
And it's had its ups and downs, but it's been very gratifying personally, because, you know I've been able to sit and write and work out my own thinking about scouting,

65
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:57,940
And then I've been able to meet people- virtually, you know, and have conversations with them about different subjects

66
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:03,159
And the response I receive indicates that other people are finding this helpful.

67
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,080
I started the podcast in February of this year and we're still rolling along.

68
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:09,820
We're at podcast number 45..

69
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:11,900
Here's what it takes to do this.

70
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:17,180
It takes a good number of hours to put together a blog and a podcast on a weekly basis

71
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:22,720
And the practical reality kind of boils down to two things:

72
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:25,660
It takes time and it takes a little tiny bit of money,

73
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:26,280
Not a lot,

74
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:27,800
Just a little bit.

75
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:33,700
Now I'll spend somewhere between 10 and 15 hours a week working on the blog and the podcast

76
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:35,300
And I'm very happy to do that.

77
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:38,580
I would probably end up doing it if nobody listened to it.

78
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,540
And did I mention I have a patient wife

79
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,660
And the expenses it's very de minimis.

80
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:52,500
It's like maybe 30 bucks a month or something like that to maintain the web services and things need for that.

81
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:53,580
So I'm not going broke

82
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:56,000
And I'm not starved for attention.

83
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:03,000
But you know, there's a tiny little bit of income that I get off of, some affiliate links and things like that on the blog.

84
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:11,700
And let me tell you how you can support the Scoutmaster blog, the Scoutmaster podcast and all the other ones that are out there,

85
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:14,260
And it's really quite simple to do.

86
00:06:14,539 --> 00:06:15,900
I'm not going to ask you for money.

87
00:06:16,219 --> 00:06:18,440
I don't really need it. to tell you the truth,

88
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:21,060
Don't tell my wife. I said that, please.

89
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,920
She's patient, but to a point

90
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,940
You know. number one is to respond to what you see and hear.

91
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:30,120
You know, respond to what you read,

92
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:38,540
Comments and questions and reviews and ratings and things like that. these mean a tremendous amount to anybody who's doing this

93
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:43,180
Because, honestly, you know there's no voices out there to respond to you.

94
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:45,400
There's no faces that you're looking at while you're doing this

95
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:49,880
And it's very easy to get into the mindset of you're kind of doing this in a vacuum.

96
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:56,300
So response in any form is tremendously important to anybody with a blog or a podcast,

97
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:58,880
And it's pretty easy to do.

98
00:06:58,980 --> 00:06:59,540
You can email,

99
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:00,900
You can comment on the blog,

100
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:03,659
You can leave ratings and reviews on iTunes-

101
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:07,000
And I mention this on every podcast just kind of as a general reminder.

102
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,840
But if you see a blog or a podcast that does have a specific business sponsor, well, do a little shopping with that sponsor

103
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:17,360
And tell them where you found out about them,

104
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:19,320
And that certainly helps.

105
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:20,600
It helps a great deal.

106
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:22,600
As I said before, I'm not going broke,

107
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:23,560
I'm not getting rich,

108
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:25,300
It's just kind of maintaining

109
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:26,560
It's all right

110
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:31,640
And I'm not really starved for attention, because people are very kind and they do respond.

111
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:36,080
If you are one of the people who respond regularly, well, I really appreciate it

112
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:37,260
And I'll thank you again.

113
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,680
If you aren't, I'd love you to get in touch soon.

114
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:41,900
You know, send me an email or something like that.

115
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:43,560
We'll talk about that at the end of the podcast.

116
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:45,840
Now we're going to talk about boards of review,

117
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:47,840
And it's a great subject

118
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:50,820
And it's something that we've got a lot of information for you on.

119
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:52,220
So let's get started. shall we?

120
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:56,060
Scout Mastership in seven minutes

121
00:07:56,860 --> 00:07:57,920
Or less.

122
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:07,900
So a scout has all his requirements signed off,

123
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:11,480
He's had his Scout Masters conference and he's ready to go to the final step,

124
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:15,460
in passing, to the next rank in scouting,

125
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:17,140
And that is a board of review.

126
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:21,160
Boards of review are kind of misunderstood in some places.

127
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:35,560
What a board of review is is not so much a retest and an examination of a scout as it is a checkup on the troop and the patrols program.

128
00:08:35,559 --> 00:08:39,959
Now this board of review will have three to six adults from the troop committee

129
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:46,380
And they will sit down with the scout and they will ask him a lot of questions for about 15 minutes

130
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:49,219
And they'll be good, open-ended questions,

131
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:53,060
So they'll be looking for more than yes or no answers.

132
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:55,940
You know what was your favorite requirement for this rank

133
00:08:55,980 --> 00:08:57,680
And can you tell us why it was your favorite?

134
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:59,080
What did you really enjoy doing?

135
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:02,980
Was there something in this that you didn't particularly enjoy doing?

136
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,620
Do you feel like you learned some things in being able to do this?

137
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:07,800
Can you tell us what you learned?

138
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:09,380
How's your patrol doing?

139
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:11,660
What would you like to do with your patrol?

140
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,260
How would you make your patrol better?

141
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:14,880
Would you like to be a patrol leader someday?

142
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:22,060
Just interesting questions to kind of investigate, to see what kind of an experience a scout is having.

143
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:24,900
The review is not of the scout.

144
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:27,640
I mean, that's not the point here.

145
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:34,220
The real point of a board of review is to find out how the troop and patrol is doing

146
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:43,480
And also to make sure that the scout is getting a good experience and that the program is working for him.

147
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:49,040
So once they've had their time, they will ask the scout to step out of the room,

148
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:51,340
They'll discuss what they have learned

149
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:55,200
And then they will ask him to come back in

150
00:09:55,800 --> 00:10:03,760
And they will, in 99.9% of the cases, congratulate him on having completed everything for his last rank.

151
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:06,880
The board of review, like I said, is not an interrogation.

152
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:09,120
It's not a retesting of a scout's competence.

153
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:10,840
It's not an examination.

154
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:18,100
It's just an attempt to see that the examinations that went into getting the scout signed off were up to standard.

155
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:23,080
It's a checkup to see that what should have been done actually was done.

156
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:25,780
It's a friendly growth experience.

157
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:30,960
Some reasons to have a board of review might include a scout's lack of advancement,

158
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:37,380
Maybe a perceived trouble that he's having in the troop, or a certain event at the last camp out or meeting.

159
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:43,000
Ideally, a scout should sit for a board of review about every six months, whether he's advancing or not.

160
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:46,860
Now, if a scout turns out to be poorly prepared for his board of review

161
00:10:46,860 --> 00:10:51,840
and maybe he has clearly not achieved what his book says that he has,

162
00:10:52,880 --> 00:11:00,960
that's a result not so much of something that the scout has done as what has brought him to the board-

163
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:05,820
those who've signed off his accomplishments without actually having them properly achieved.

164
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:11,900
Thus, a scout might not be as responsible for his lack of preparation as one would think.

165
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:16,380
Now, this doesn't wipe away all the responsibility on the scout,

166
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:19,980
but it does place responsibility where it ought to be-

167
00:11:20,319 --> 00:11:22,439
those who are delivering the program to him.

168
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:26,980
The actual meeting with the boy should be give and take,

169
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:30,360
an informal conversation between the scout and a group of adults

170
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:34,900
who are interested in his welfare and are supportive of his efforts.

171
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:38,840
We should encourage the scout to come to conclusions on his own

172
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:41,820
and not just tell them what we think.

173
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:47,200
What we have to do to keep our troop boards of review on track

174
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:52,560
is to see that the members of the board have been properly trained to conduct a board of review,

175
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:57,580
And there is an excellent resource, a board of review training,

176
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:02,680
and there's a PDF version of that that I'll link to on the blog.

177
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:06,540
And there are many, many resources available on the web,

178
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:12,979
But I would make sure to say: be sure that they are consistent with BSA training.

179
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:19,939
Ask Andy, which is a venerable web-based advice column for scouts,

180
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:24,859
has a great true-false quiz on a board of review, and I'll include a link to that.

181
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:30,839
I just thought it might be interesting for us to go through a few of the questions from that true-false quiz

182
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:38,120
and we'll get an idea how good we are at understanding the intent and the conduct of a board of review.

183
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:40,280
Here is question number one:

184
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:42,520
True or false? You ready?

185
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:49,820
The board of review is the final opportunity for the troop's adult leaders to retest the scout,

186
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:55,780
to make certain he's mastered the skills and learned the requirements for the rank that he's seeking.

187
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:58,700
So what do you think, True or false?

188
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:02,660
Well, that's false because we are not retesting the skills.

189
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:05,360
We want to know that he's learned them.

190
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
We want to know that he has a legitimate claim to having those things signed off.

191
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:13,180
But it doesn't really mean that we retest the skills.

192
00:13:14,340 --> 00:13:15,860
Now, what would a retest be?

193
00:13:16,020 --> 00:13:17,480
Well, tie me these knots,

194
00:13:18,260 --> 00:13:20,760
Show me this first aid problem,

195
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:24,780
Explain the whole map and compass thing to me.

196
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:25,760
Something like that.

197
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:29,460
That really doesn't have a place in a board of review, because those skills have already happened.

198
00:13:30,120 --> 00:13:33,560
Now we might ask some questions about those.

199
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:37,020
What can you tell me? what a square knot is used for?

200
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:43,819
Or tell me how you completed your first aid requirement for the badge?

201
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:45,600
Explain to me how that worked-

202
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:49,600
And this is just kind of a checkup, not to see that the scout did everything properly,

203
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:52,720
but to see that the troop and patrol did everything properly.

204
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:55,600
I do this myself as a scoutmaster. every once in a while

205
00:13:55,800 --> 00:14:00,140
I'll just you know, in a scoutmaster's conference I will ask the same type of questions

206
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:05,060
to check up and make sure that whoever is signing off requirements

207
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:09,020
has gone ahead and done that properly.

208
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:14,400
I will sign off requirements for a boy's badge of rank, maybe once a month.

209
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:19,720
Most of that happens with patrol leaders and the senior patrol leader and the older scouts

210
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:24,140
in the troop are the ones signing those requirements off, and my assistant scoutmasters as well,

211
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:26,500
but largely youth members.

212
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:31,900
Inevitably, there's going to be a point at which I need to remind them to read the requirements

213
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:32,660
very carefully.

214
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:34,560
Okay, so this one is tricky.

215
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:35,860
This question is tricky:

216
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:36,579
True or false?

217
00:14:36,580 --> 00:14:37,060
False.

218
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:43,560
The scoutmasters or assistant scoutmasters vote in a board of review carries the same weight

219
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:44,420
as all the others,

220
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:46,540
True or false?

221
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:48,240
You got that one easy right.

222
00:14:48,820 --> 00:14:52,400
Scoutmasters and assistant scoutmasters do not sit on boards of review.

223
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:54,480
We know that right.

224
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,740
I mean, that's a policy statement from the BSA.

225
00:14:59,940 --> 00:15:05,020
Scoutmasters and assistant scoutmasters do not participate in boards of review.

226
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:07,880
A couple more quick true-false questions.

227
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:08,480
How about this?

228
00:15:08,819 --> 00:15:13,780
A group board of review can be conducted for multiple scouts simultaneously, so long as they're

229
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:15,140
all candidates for the same rank.

230
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:19,600
Well, that's false.

231
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,900
You don't have multiple boards of review.

232
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:27,180
This is a personal growth agreement conference with an individual scout,

233
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:33,000
And you really can't. you don't do this with three guys at a time, or five guys at a time,

234
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,160
or two guys at a time.

235
00:15:34,579 --> 00:15:40,760
This is that scout's opportunity to sit down with members of the troop committee and to

236
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:43,099
discuss his career in scouting with them.

